WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.940
Welcome to Grade 6 English, Unit 4: Inventions and Creativity.

2
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:06.800
Welcome to Unit 4!

3
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:14.460
This unit is all about inventions and creativity — two
powerful forces that have shaped the world we live in today.

4
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.260
Throughout history, people have looked at problems
around them and imagined new ways to solve them.

5
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:28.600
From the simple wheel to the complex machines of the modern
age, every invention began as an idea in somebody’s mind.

6
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:35.560
In this unit, you will explore how inventions
work, why they matter, and how ordinary people —

7
00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.560
including young people just like you — have
changed the world with creative thinking.

8
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:46.500
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

9
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:53.600
By the end of this unit, you will be able to use
vocabulary related to inventions, exploration, science,

10
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.640
and technology confidently in speech and writing.

11
00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:04.200
you will also read and understand an informational passage
about inventions and answer comprehension questions about it.

12
00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:10.560
you will also identify the past simple tense
in sentences and explain when and why we use it.

13
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.840
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

14
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:21.700
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: straits,
explorer, excursion, caravan, territory, navigation.

15
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:27.540
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

16
00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.300
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

17
00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:40.910
The reading sequence includes Inventions and Creativity:
source text 1 and Inventions and Creativity: source text 2.

18
00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:42.220
Read actively.

19
00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:47.200
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

20
00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:51.300
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

21
00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:55.520
The first language focus is Past Simple Tense (Reviewed).

22
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:03.800
What it means and why it matters The past simple tense is
used to talk about actions or events that happened in the past

23
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.060
and are now finished.

24
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.660
It tells us that something occurred
at a specific time and is complete.

25
00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:16.080
For example, “The explorer claimed a new route”
tells us this action happened in the past.

26
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:23.260
Knowing how to use the past simple helps us share
stories, describe experiences, write about history,

27
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.240
and communicate about things that have already happened.

28
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.920
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

29
00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:40.320
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Vocabulary Expert Interview.

30
00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:45.560
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

31
00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.560
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

32
00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.160
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

33
00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:54.980
You are ready to begin.

34
00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:02.060
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

35
00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.200
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

36
00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:11.720
Use AI English for hints and feedback, and
bring your questions to the live sessions.
